/**
 * Briefing lessons: ≤5 short paragraphs per mission, in the mentor's voice, tying the concept cards
 * to the ticket (what is happening, which tool, how to use it, when it matters). Content rule in
 * AGENTS.md §5; enforced by tests/missions/content.test.ts.
 */
export const LESSONS: Record<string, string[]> = {
  '0.1': [
    'Everything you will ever do here goes through one door: the API server. `kubectl` is just a client that asks it questions (`get`, `describe`) or hands it desired state (`apply`). The control plane decides what should run; the nodes — the four racks on the map — actually run it. When you type a command, watch the map: it is the same data the API server has.',
    'Start wide, then narrow. `kubectl get nodes` tells you what the cluster is made of. `kubectl get pods -A` lists every Pod in every namespace — `-A` is the flag you reach for when you do not yet know where something lives. Pods are grouped into **namespaces** (freight, billing…); without `-n` you only see the `default` one, which is often empty and misleading.',
    'When a row looks interesting, zoom in with `kubectl describe pod NAME -n NAMESPACE`. The bottom of that output — **Events** — is where the kubelet and the scheduler tell you what they did and why. During incidents this is the first thing you read.',
    'Tab completes resource types and names, so you rarely type a full Pod name. Short names save keystrokes: `po`, `deploy`, `svc`, `ns`. Use them; every engineer on the floor does.',
  ],
  '1.1': [
    'A Pod runs **images**, and an image is addressed as `registry/repository:tag`. The registry is where the bytes live, the tag is a human label for a version. CI builds images with Docker and pushes them; the node\'s runtime (containerd) pulls them when the Pod is scheduled. The two halves never talk to each other except through that name — so a typo in the tag means the node asks for something that does not exist.',
    'That failure has a signature. First `ErrImagePull` (the pull itself failed), then `ImagePullBackOff` (the kubelet is waiting before retrying, doubling the delay up to five minutes). `kubectl get pods -n freight` shows it in the STATUS column; `kubectl describe pod NAME -n freight` shows the registry\'s actual error in the Events, which is what you need to decide whether the tag, the repository or the credentials are wrong.',
    'Do not fix the Pod. Pods owned by a Deployment are replaced from the Deployment\'s **template**; deleting them just makes identical broken ones. Change the template instead: `kubectl set image deployment/cargo-api cargo-api=IMAGE -n freight`, or `kubectl edit deployment cargo-api -n freight`. The controller then rolls out new Pods with the corrected image.',
    'Confirm with `kubectl rollout status deployment/cargo-api -n freight` and a final `kubectl get pods`. You will see this exact pattern — wrong tag, private registry without credentials, image deleted from the registry — more often than any other startup failure, so learn the signature now.',
  ],
  '2.1': [
    'Clusters are shared. Teams get **namespaces**, and almost every object you touch lives in one. Your kubeconfig context has a default namespace; commands without `-n` go there. So "I cannot see the pod" is usually "I am looking in the wrong namespace". `-A` searches all of them at once, which is how you find a thing when the alert forgot to say where it is.',
    'A crash loop has a tell: STATUS `CrashLoopBackOff` and a RESTARTS count that keeps climbing. `kubectl get pods -A` is enough to spot it. Add `-o wide` and you also get the Pod IP and the **node** it runs on — the two facts people ask for first when you report an incident.',
    '**Labels** are how Kubernetes groups things: Services, ReplicaSets and your own queries all select by label. `--show-labels` reveals them, `-l key=value` filters on them, and `kubectl label pod NAME key=value` adds one. Tagging a Pod with `triage=p1` is a lightweight way to hand it to another team without touching its spec.',
    'Finally, stop fighting `-n`: `kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=billing` changes the default for every following command. Do it whenever you are going to spend more than a few commands in one namespace.',
  ],
  '2.2': [
    'A Pod that keeps restarting is telling you something — in its logs. `kubectl logs POD -n freight` prints the container\'s stdout/stderr. In a crash loop the *current* container may be seconds old and silent; `--previous` (`-p`) prints the logs of the one that just died, which is where the fatal error is. Read that before anything else.',
    'Then confirm the mechanics with `kubectl describe pod`: **Last State: Terminated, Exit Code 1** means the application exited on purpose (a config or dependency error); 137 means it was killed (out of memory, failed liveness probe). The Events at the bottom show `Back-off restarting failed container`, the kubelet\'s way of saying "I keep trying".',
    'Here the fix is an environment variable. You could patch the live Deployment with `kubectl set env`, but the manifest in `~/manifests/` is what CI applies, and the next apply would silently remove your change. So edit the manifest (`vim manifests/cargo-api.yaml`), add the variable under the container\'s `env:` list, and `kubectl apply -f` it — the same command CI runs.',
    'Apply computes the difference and rolls out new Pods. Watch it finish with `kubectl rollout status deployment/cargo-api -n freight`, then check endpoints or `get pods` to be sure traffic is served again. Logs → describe → fix the source of truth → verify: that is the shape of most incidents you will handle.',
  ],
  '2.3': [
    'There are two ways to talk to the API server. **Imperative** commands (`kubectl run`, `create`, `scale`, `set image`) tell it what to do right now. **Declarative** means writing the desired state in a YAML file and letting `kubectl apply -f` make the cluster match it. Imperative is fast for experiments; declarative is what you commit to git and what CI reproduces on every environment.',
    'Hand-made objects — like the `notifier-manual` Pod in this ticket — are drift: nothing owns them, nobody can review them, and a node failure deletes them for good. The remedy is to turn them into manifests. You do not have to write YAML from scratch: `kubectl create deployment NAME --image=IMG --replicas=2 --dry-run=client -o yaml` prints a correct manifest without creating anything. Redirect it to a file and edit from there.',
    '`kubectl apply -f FILE` creates the object the first time and updates it afterwards. It remembers what you applied in the `last-applied-configuration` annotation and uses it to compute a three-way diff, which is why re-applying the same file prints `unchanged` and why changing `replicas:` in the file and applying again is the correct way to scale something that is managed declaratively.',
    'Once the Deployment exists, delete the unmanaged Pod (`kubectl delete pod notifier-manual`). From now on, when someone asks "how many replicas does notifier have", the answer is in the file.',
  ],
  '2.4': [
    'Sometimes you need to see the world from inside a Pod: which environment variables it got, whether it can reach another service, what its DNS resolves to. `kubectl exec POD -- CMD` runs a command in the container; `kubectl exec -it POD -- sh` gives you an interactive shell. The `--` separates kubectl\'s own flags from the command you want to run.',
    'Inside the cluster, Service names resolve through cluster DNS: `frontend` from the same namespace, `frontend.freight` or `frontend.freight.svc.cluster.local` from anywhere. So `kubectl exec tools -- curl -s http://frontend/healthz` is a complete end-to-end test of a Service, its endpoints and the application behind it.',
    'From your workstation those names do not resolve, because you are outside the cluster network. `kubectl port-forward deploy/frontend 8080:8080` tunnels a local port to a Pod through the API server. It blocks the terminal, so run it in the background with `&` (stop it later with `kill %1`) and then `curl localhost:8080`.',
    'exec, port-forward and logs are your three debugging windows into a running application. They need no changes to the app and no exposure to the outside world — which is why you should reach for them before you reach for a NodePort.',
  ],
  '3.1': [
    'You will delete a Pod in this ticket and it will come back within seconds. That is not magic; it is a **ReplicaSet** doing its one job: keep the number of Pods matching its label selector equal to `spec.replicas`. Watch the map after `kubectl delete pod`, then look at `kubectl get rs -n freight` and `kubectl describe rs NAME` — the `SuccessfulCreate` event is the controller replacing what you removed.',
    'This observe-compare-act loop is the **controller pattern**, and all of Kubernetes is built on it. You rarely create ReplicaSets yourself: a **Deployment** creates one per Pod template revision and manages the rollout between them. That is why the ReplicaSet name is `frontend-<hash>` — the hash identifies the template.',
    'Scaling is therefore a one-liner on the Deployment: `kubectl scale deployment frontend --replicas=5 -n freight`. The Deployment updates its ReplicaSet, the ReplicaSet creates Pods, the scheduler places them, and the Service starts routing to each one the moment it is Ready. Check `kubectl get deploy` until READY shows 5/5.',
    'Traffic is live, so the SLO meter is your score. Deleting a Pod under load is safe only because the other replicas keep serving while the replacement starts — which is also the whole argument for never running a single replica of anything that matters.',
  ],
  '3.2': [
    'Releasing a new version means changing the Deployment\'s Pod template — usually the image. `kubectl set image deployment/frontend frontend=IMAGE -n freight` does exactly that. The controller creates a new ReplicaSet and shifts replicas across, adding at most `maxSurge` extra Pods and allowing at most `maxUnavailable` missing ones (25% each by default). Old Pods are removed only once new ones are **available**, so a healthy rollout never drops traffic.',
    '`kubectl rollout status deployment/frontend -n freight` follows the rollout and prints the controller\'s progress until it says `successfully rolled out`. Use it every time; it is also the command you wrap in CI to block a pipeline on a failed deploy.',
    'Every template change is a **revision**, kept as an old ReplicaSet scaled to zero. `kubectl rollout history deployment/frontend` lists them. If a release is bad, `kubectl rollout undo deployment/frontend` goes back one revision (or `--to-revision=N`) — and a rollback is just another rolling update, with the same safety rules.',
    'Here is the comforting part: if new Pods crash on start, the rollout stalls at the `maxUnavailable` boundary and most old Pods keep serving. Your job is to notice (`get pods` shows the crash-looping new ones), undo, and verify. Stay on the console after the first rollout finishes; CI has been busy.',
  ],
  '3.3': [
    'A container being *Running* does not mean the application inside is ready to serve. This frontend needs twelve seconds to warm its cache, and in that window it answers 503. Without a **readiness probe**, Kubernetes considers the Pod Ready the instant the process starts, adds it to the Service endpoints, and your customers get the 503s. With one, the Pod stays out of the endpoints until `/readyz` returns 200.',
    'Probes are declared per container in the manifest, next to `image:`: `readinessProbe: {httpGet: {path: /readyz, port: 8080}, periodSeconds: 5}`. A **liveness probe** (`/healthz`) is different in consequence: when it fails, the kubelet restarts the container. Readiness says "do not send me traffic yet"; liveness says "I am stuck, restart me". Startup probes exist for very slow starters.',
    'Timing matters. `periodSeconds` (default 10), `failureThreshold` (3) and `initialDelaySeconds` (0) decide how fast a Pod enters or leaves service. A readiness period of 5s means at most ~5s between the app becoming ready and traffic arriving. Aggressive liveness probes on a slow app cause restart storms — give liveness a longer delay than readiness.',
    'Rollouts respect this too: the Deployment only retires an old Pod when a new one is **available** (Ready for `minReadySeconds`). That is why the fix must go into the manifest and be applied: the rolling update you trigger is itself the proof that the probes work. Apply, watch `rollout status`, and keep the SLO green.',
  ],
  '3.4': [
    'Memory is the resource that kills. A container\'s **limit** is a hard ceiling: exceed it and the kernel OOM-kills the process (exit code 137, reason `OOMKilled`), the kubelet restarts it, and you get a crash loop that looks like a bug but is a number. `kubectl describe pod` shows the limit under the container and `Last State: Terminated, Reason: OOMKilled`.',
    '**Requests** are different: they are what the scheduler reserves when placing the Pod. A Pod only lands on a node with enough unreserved memory for its request. Ask for more than any node can give and the Pod stays Pending with a `FailedScheduling` event: `0/4 nodes are available: 3 Insufficient memory`. `kubectl describe node` shows what is already allocated.',
    'So sizing is two numbers: a limit comfortably above the real peak (this app needs ~312 MiB), and a request that fits the nodes (2 Gi workers here, with other things on them). Requests must be ≤ limits. `kubectl set resources deployment/scheduler --limits=memory=512Mi --requests=memory=384Mi -n freight` sets both and triggers a rollout.',
    'The combination determines the **QoS class**: requests equal to limits is Guaranteed, anything else set is Burstable, nothing set is BestEffort — evicted first under node pressure. Check it with `describe` or `-o jsonpath=\'{.status.qosClass}\'`. Right-sizing is routine work; `kubectl top pods` (metrics-server) gives you real usage to base it on.',
  ],
  '3.5': [
    'Not everything is a long-running server. A **Job** runs Pods until a number of them succeed: `completions` says how many successes, `parallelism` how many run at once, `backoffLimit` how many failures to tolerate. Job Pods use `restartPolicy: Never` or `OnFailure`. Generate one with `kubectl create job NAME --image=IMG --dry-run=client -o yaml`, add `completions`/`parallelism` under `spec`, apply, then `kubectl get jobs` and `kubectl logs job/NAME`.',
    'A **CronJob** creates Jobs on a schedule written in cron syntax; `*/2 * * * *` is every two minutes, which lets you watch one fire during this ticket. `kubectl create cronjob NAME --image=IMG --schedule="*/2 * * * *"` is the one-liner. `concurrencyPolicy` decides what happens if the previous run is still going; history limits prune old Jobs.',
    'A **DaemonSet** runs exactly one Pod on every matching node — log shippers, monitoring agents, network plugins. You never scale it; the cluster does. Apply the manifest, then `kubectl get ds` and `kubectl get pods -o wide` to see one agent per worker. When a new node joins, a Pod appears on it with no action from you; cordoning a node does not remove it.',
    'Three controllers, three shapes of work: run-to-completion, scheduled, and one-per-node. Knowing which one fits saves you from writing a server that only loops over a cron.',
  ],
  '3.6': [
    'This is the full shift. Two releases, a new platform policy, then infrastructure work — every tool from this chapter in one ticket. Start with the manifests in `~/manifests/`: bump both image tags, add readiness (`/readyz`) and liveness (`/healthz`) probes on port 8080, and `resources.limits.memory` for each container. Apply them and watch `rollout status` for each Deployment; with probes in place the rollouts are zero-downtime by construction.',
    'Then a node dies. A NotReady node gets `node.kubernetes.io/unreachable` taints; its Pods are marked NotReady immediately — traffic stops flowing to them — but they are only **evicted** after `tolerationSeconds` (this cluster: 40s). Until then the ReplicaSet counts them as present and creates nothing. Your SLO survives only if the remaining replicas can carry the load; scale up if they cannot, then let eviction do its work.',
    'Maintenance is the opposite: a planned departure. `kubectl drain NODE --ignore-daemonsets` cordons the node (no new Pods) and evicts every Pod on it so controllers recreate them elsewhere; DaemonSet Pods are skipped. `kubectl get nodes` shows `SchedulingDisabled`. When infra is done, `kubectl uncordon NODE` returns it to the scheduler.',
    'Keep three frontends ready throughout. That is the real objective: everything else is the mechanism.',
  ],
  '4.1': [
    'A **Service** is a stable name and virtual IP in front of a set of Pods. Which Pods? The ones whose labels match `spec.selector`. That match produces the Service\'s **endpoints**; kube-proxy forwards connections to them. No matching ready Pods means no endpoints, and every connection is refused — while the Pods themselves look perfectly healthy.',
    'So the first command is `kubectl get endpoints cargo-api -n freight`. `<none>` is the whole diagnosis. Then compare the two sides: `kubectl get svc cargo-api -o wide` (SELECTOR column) against `kubectl get pods --show-labels`. Every selector entry must match (AND): adding a matching key while keeping a stale key still selects no Pods. The selector must match the **Pod** labels, which come from the Deployment\'s template — not the Deployment\'s own labels.',
    'From inside the cluster you can prove it: `kubectl exec tools -- nslookup cargo-api` resolves (the Service exists), `kubectl exec tools -- curl -s cargo-api/healthz` fails (no endpoints). DNS answering but connections failing is the fingerprint of a selector mismatch — or of a readiness probe failing, the other usual suspect.',
    'Fix either side, consistently: replace the old entry under `spec.selector` (`kubectl edit svc cargo-api -n freight`) or change the Pod labels in the Deployment template. Save and Resume if paused so the endpoints controller can react; `get endpoints` will show two addresses and the map will show traffic flowing again.',
  ],
  '4.2': [
    'Three Service types, each building on the last. **ClusterIP** (default) is reachable only inside the cluster. **NodePort** also opens the same port (30000–32767) on every node, so anything that can reach a node IP can reach the Service. **LoadBalancer** asks the cloud for an external IP that forwards to that NodePort. `kubectl expose deployment frontend --type=NodePort --port=80 --target-port=8080 --name=frontend-nodeport` creates one from the Deployment\'s selector.',
    'Read the port columns carefully: `port` is the Service\'s own port (what clients inside use), `targetPort` is the container port traffic is delivered to, `nodePort` is the per-node port. `kubectl get svc` prints `80:31234/TCP` meaning port 80, nodePort 31234. Get a node\'s INTERNAL-IP from `kubectl get nodes -o wide` and `curl NODE_IP:31234`.',
    'A LoadBalancer Service shows `<pending>` under EXTERNAL-IP until the cloud controller provisions it — seconds here, minutes in real clouds, forever on bare metal without something like MetalLB. `kubectl get svc frontend-lb -w` watches until the IP appears; then `curl EXTERNAL_IP` on port 80.',
    'For real traffic you will put an Ingress or Gateway in front of one LoadBalancer and route by host and path; that is the next chapter. NodePort and LoadBalancer are the primitives it is built on, so you should see them work once with your own eyes.',
  ],
  '1.2': [
    'An image is not a version; it is a stack of read-only **layers** with a content hash, the **digest**. A tag such as `1.2.0` or `latest` is a label that points at a digest — and the registry lets anyone with push rights move it. CI pushed a development build under `latest` last night. Nodes that already ran the old one never noticed; the node that started a fresh container asked the registry what `latest` means today.',
    'So three Pods with the identical image line run two programs. The proof is in the Pod status, not the spec: `kubectl describe pods -n freight -l app=notifier | grep "Image ID"` prints the digest each container was actually started from. Two different `sha256:` values behind one tag is the whole diagnosis.',
    'The rule that follows: never deploy a tag that moves. Pin a released version (`kubectl set image deployment/notifier notifier=…/notifier:1.2.0 -n freight`), or the digest itself in pipelines. `imagePullPolicy` only decides *when* a node asks the registry — `Always` for `latest`, `IfNotPresent` otherwise — it cannot make a mutable tag reproducible.',
    'One more thing worth seeing once: `kubectl get nodes -o wide` shows the CONTAINER-RUNTIME, containerd. Images are built elsewhere, from a Dockerfile, usually in CI; the nodes only pull and run them through the Container Runtime Interface. Docker on your laptop and the runtime on the node meet at exactly one place: the registry.',
  ],
  '3.7': [
    'A Pod is not a container; it is a small group of containers that share a network identity and, when you ask for it, files. That is what makes the **sidecar** pattern possible: a helper that runs next to an application you cannot or should not change. Here the application insists on writing its audit trail to a file, while everything downstream reads stdout.',
    'Start by seeing the problem. `kubectl logs deploy/archiver -n freight` shows one line; `kubectl exec deploy/archiver -n freight -- cat /var/log/archiver/audit.log` shows the real log, inside the container\'s private filesystem where no other container can reach it.',
    'The bridge is a volume. Declare `volumes: [{name: logs, emptyDir: {}}]` in the Pod spec and mount it at `/var/log/archiver` in the archiver container: the file now lands in storage that belongs to the Pod. Add a second container (`busybox:1.36`) that mounts the same volume and runs `tail -n+1 -F /var/log/archiver/audit.log`. Its stdout *is* the audit trail: `kubectl logs deploy/archiver -c audit-tail -n freight`.',
    'Two details matter. `-c` selects the container, and READY becomes `2/2` — both must be up for the Pod to be Ready. And a sidecar shares the Pod\'s fate: when the Pod is deleted the `emptyDir` goes with it. For helpers that must start before the app and stop after it, declare them under `initContainers` with `restartPolicy: Always` — a native sidecar.',
  ],
  '3.8': [
    'Hardened images are often **distroless**: the binary, its libraries, nothing else. No shell means `kubectl exec POD -- sh` answers `executable file not found in $PATH`. Try it first — recognising that error saves you from suspecting the cluster.',
    'The tool for this is an **ephemeral container**: `kubectl debug POD -n freight -it --image=busybox:1.36 --target=route-planner`. Kubernetes adds a temporary container to the running Pod. It brings its own tools and shares two things with the application: the network namespace, so `localhost:9090` is the planner\'s admin port, and — because of `--target` — the process namespace, so `ps` lists the planner and `/proc/1/root/` is its filesystem.',
    'From that shell, ask the application what it believes: `wget -qO- localhost:9090/debug/config`, or `cat /proc/1/environ`. Compare the upstream it prints with what exists: `kubectl get svc -n freight`. A name that resolves nowhere is a 502 waiting for every request.',
    'Then leave the debug container alone — it cannot be removed and disappears with the Pod — and fix the source: `kubectl set env deployment/route-planner CARGO_API_HOST=… -n freight`. The rollout replaces the Pods, debug container included. Look with ephemeral containers; change with Deployments.',
  ],
  '4.3': [
    'A LoadBalancer per Service gets expensive and tells you nothing about hosts or paths. An **Ingress** describes HTTP routing — this host, this path, that Service — and an **ingress controller** turns those rules into proxy configuration behind a single load balancer. The Ingress object alone does nothing; `ingressClassName` says which controller should act on it. Find yours: `kubectl get ingressclass`, and its address with `kubectl get svc -n ingress-nginx`.',
    'TLS first. The controller terminates HTTPS using a Secret of type `kubernetes.io/tls` in the Ingress\'s namespace: `kubectl create secret tls freight-tls --cert=certs/freight.crt --key=certs/freight.key -n freight`. Reference it under `spec.tls` together with the host. If the Secret is missing or misnamed, ingress-nginx still answers — with its self-signed "Fake Certificate" — and every client that verifies certificates refuses to talk.',
    'Then the rules: one host, two `Prefix` paths. `/api` to cargo-api and `/` to frontend, both on Service port 80; the longest matching prefix wins, so order in the file does not matter. `kubectl create ingress` can write it for you, and `kubectl describe ingress freight -n freight` shows each backend with its endpoints — `<none>` there is a 503 for that path.',
    'Test before DNS exists: the proxy routes on the hostname the client sends. `curl --resolve freight.orbital.example:443:LB_IP https://freight.orbital.example/` connects to the address you choose while presenting the right name for SNI and `Host`. Do not reach for `-k`: an error about the certificate is information.',
  ],
  '4.4': [
    'Ingress put everything in one object and grew by annotation. The **Gateway API** splits the job by role. A **GatewayClass** names the implementation (here `eg`, Envoy Gateway). A **Gateway** is the load balancer itself — listeners, ports, TLS, and which namespaces may use it — owned by the platform team. An **HTTPRoute** carries hostnames, matches and backends, owned by you. Look at what exists: `kubectl get gatewayclass`, `kubectl get gateway -A`.',
    'Attachment is explicit on both sides. Your route points at the Gateway with `parentRefs` (name and, across namespaces, namespace); the listener decides whether to accept it through `allowedRoutes`. The verdict is written into the route\'s status: `kubectl describe httproute frontend -n freight` and read `Accepted` and `ResolvedRefs`. A route that is not accepted routes nothing, and the proxy answers 404.',
    'Traffic splitting is a first-class field. Each rule lists `backendRefs`; give them `weight` and the proxy divides requests proportionally. 90 for `frontend` and 10 for `frontend-next` is a canary: a tenth of real users exercise the new version while the rest are unaffected. Changing weights restarts nothing.',
    'A canary is a question — "is the new version as good under real traffic?" — so wait for an answer before promoting: error rate and latency of the canary backend. Then set the weights to 0 and 100. The previous version stays deployed until you remove it, which makes the rollback one `kubectl apply` away.',
  ],
  '4.5': [
    'By default every Pod can connect to every other Pod, in any namespace. That is convenient until someone compromises one container. A **NetworkPolicy** changes the default for the Pods its `podSelector` matches: once any ingress policy selects a Pod, only connections some policy explicitly allows are accepted. There are no deny rules; you deny by not allowing.',
    'Prove the finding first: `kubectl exec tools -n freight -- nc -z cargo-db 6379` succeeds. Then read the two files in `~/policies/`. `default-deny` selects every Pod (`podSelector: {}`) and lists no rules. `allow-frontend` opens port 8080 of `app=frontend` to any source. You need two more: cargo-api from `app=frontend` on 8080, cargo-db from `app=cargo-api` on 6379 — a `from` with a `podSelector`, plus `ports`.',
    'Order is the whole game on a live system. Applied alone, default-deny cuts customers off from frontend and frontend from cargo-api. Applied together with the allow rules — `kubectl apply -f policies/` — nobody notices. Policies are additive, so getting an allow rule in early is always safe.',
    'Verify like an attacker: the same `nc` from the tools pod, with `-w 3`, now hangs and fails. A blocked connection **times out**; "connection refused" would mean the packet arrived and nothing listened. And remember who enforces this: the CNI plugin. This cluster runs Cilium; on a CNI without policy support the API accepts your YAML and nothing happens.',
  ],
  '4.6': [
    'Everything from this chapter in one go-live. Build from the outside in, and check each layer\'s status before leaning on it. First the certificate: a `kubernetes.io/tls` Secret named `freight-tls` in freight. Then the Gateway: add an HTTPS listener on 443 with `tls.certificateRefs` to that Secret, apply, and wait for `kubectl get gateway -n freight` to show an ADDRESS and PROGRAMMED `True`.',
    'Apply the HTTPRoute and confirm it is `Accepted`. Now test the whole path the way a customer would: `curl --resolve freight.orbital.example:443:ADDRESS https://freight.orbital.example/` and `/api/cargo`. Only when both answer do you have something worth protecting.',
    'Segmentation changes one idea from the previous ticket: the clients of frontend and cargo-api are no longer "the internet" but the Gateway\'s **proxy Pods**, which live in `envoy-gateway-system`. A `namespaceSelector` on `kubernetes.io/metadata.name` allows exactly them. cargo-db still accepts only cargo-api. Write the allow policies, then apply the directory so they land together with the default-deny.',
    'Finish as an outsider: from the tools pod, `nc -z -w 3` to cargo-api and cargo-db must time out, while the SLO meter stays green. TLS at the edge, least privilege behind it — that is the shape of almost every production front door.',
  ],
  '5.1': [
    'An image that carries its own settings has to be rebuilt for every environment and every password rotation. Kubernetes separates the two: configuration lives in **ConfigMaps**, credentials in **Secrets**, and the Pod spec says how the container receives them. Start with the symptom — `kubectl logs deploy/notifier -n freight --previous` lists what the process could not find.',
    'Create the objects imperatively: `kubectl create configmap notifier-config --from-literal=mode=batch -n freight`; `kubectl create configmap notifier-templates --from-file=config/templates.yaml -n freight` (the file name becomes the key); `kubectl create secret generic smtp-credentials --from-literal=password=… -n freight`.',
    'Then wire them into the Deployment by reference, never by value. For variables: `env` entries with `valueFrom.configMapKeyRef` or `secretKeyRef`. For the file: a `volume` of type `configMap` plus a `volumeMount` at `/etc/notifier` — each key appears as a file. A missing ConfigMap or key shows up as `CreateContainerConfigError` on the Pod, which is your cue to compare names.',
    'Two facts to carry with you. A Secret is base64-encoded, not encrypted: `kubectl get secret smtp-credentials -n freight -o jsonpath=\'{.data.password}\' | base64 -d` prints it, so protect Secrets with RBAC and keep them out of git. And environment variables are a snapshot taken when the container starts: after you change a ConfigMap, running Pods keep the old value until `kubectl rollout restart`. Mounted files do follow, within a minute.',
  ],
  '5.2': [
    'A container\'s filesystem dies with the container. Anything that must survive — a ledger, a database — belongs on a **PersistentVolume**, and a Pod gets one by naming a **PersistentVolumeClaim**. The claim states what you need (5Gi, `ReadWriteOnce`); a **StorageClass** names the CSI provisioner that creates the volume and binds it. Check the logs first: the ledger itself warns that its data directory is not a mounted volume.',
    'Apply the claim your colleague left and look at it: `kubectl get pvc -n freight` says `Pending`. `kubectl describe pvc ledger-data -n freight` has the reason in its events, and `kubectl get sc` shows which classes this cluster really offers — one of them is marked `(default)` and is used when a claim names none.',
    'A claim\'s class cannot be edited after creation. Delete the claim, fix the manifest, apply again; within seconds it is `Bound` to a freshly provisioned volume named `pvc-<uid>`.',
    'Now connect it: in the Deployment, a `volumes` entry with `persistentVolumeClaim.claimName: ledger-data` and a `volumeMounts` entry at `/var/lib/ledger`. Because the volume is `ReadWriteOnce`, the Deployment uses `strategy: Recreate` — old Pod out before new Pod in. Then run the test Finance asked for: delete the Pod, and read the new Pod\'s first log lines. "recovered N entries" is what durability looks like.',
  ],
  '5.3': [
    'Deployments treat Pods as interchangeable: random names, any order, shared or no storage. Clustered datastores need the opposite, and a **StatefulSet** provides it: Pods named by ordinal (`routecache-0`, `-1`, `-2`), started one after another, each bound to its own PersistentVolumeClaim created from `volumeClaimTemplates`. `kubectl get pods,pvc -n freight` shows the pairs.',
    'Clients address members by name, and those names come from DNS. A StatefulSet\'s `serviceName` must point at a **headless Service** — `clusterIP: None` — which makes cluster DNS publish one record per Pod: `routecache-0.routecache.freight.svc.cluster.local`. The StatefulSet does not create that Service and does not complain when it is missing; the Pods run, the names just do not exist. That is this ticket: read `kubectl logs deploy/planner-worker -n freight --previous`.',
    'Create it — `kubectl create service clusterip routecache --clusterip=None --tcp=6379:6379 -n freight` — and test from the tools pod with `nslookup routecache-0.routecache`. A crash-looping client waits out its back-off; `kubectl rollout restart deployment/planner-worker` spares you the wait.',
    'Then watch the ordering guarantees. `kubectl scale sts routecache --replicas=5` creates `-3`, waits until it is Ready, then `-4`, each with a new claim. Delete `routecache-1` and it returns with the same name and the same claim: identity is the name, state is the disk, and the StatefulSet keeps them together.',
  ],
  '5.4': [
    'Every request to the API server is made by someone. For Pods that someone is a **ServiceAccount**: `system:serviceaccount:<namespace>:<name>`, `default` unless the Pod says otherwise. RBAC then decides what it may do by adding up grants: **Roles** (verbs on resources in one namespace) or **ClusterRoles**, attached to subjects by **RoleBindings** or **ClusterRoleBindings**. Nothing is ever subtracted.',
    'Ask before you touch anything: `kubectl auth can-i delete nodes --as=system:serviceaccount:freight:default`. A `yes` there means every Pod in freight that did not choose another account holds cluster-admin. `kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o wide` shows the binding responsible.',
    'Removing it first would break the agent, so build the replacement first: `kubectl create serviceaccount fleet-agent -n freight`; `kubectl create role pod-reader --verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods -n freight`; `kubectl create rolebinding … --role=pod-reader --serviceaccount=freight:fleet-agent -n freight`. Check it with `kubectl auth can-i list pods -n freight --as=system:serviceaccount:freight:fleet-agent` — and with something it must not do.',
    'Move the workload: `kubectl set serviceaccount deployment/fleet-agent fleet-agent -n freight`, wait for the rollout, and only then delete the ClusterRoleBinding. Grant, switch, revoke: in that order nobody loses access for a second. The same three commands, with a user or group as subject, are how humans get access too.',
  ],
  '5.5': [
    'By default a container runs as whatever user its image says — often root — with a writable filesystem and a set of Linux capabilities it almost never needs. A **securityContext** removes that: `runAsNonRoot`, `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`, `capabilities.drop: ["ALL"]`, `seccompProfile.type: RuntimeDefault`, `readOnlyRootFilesystem`. None of it changes the application; all of it limits what an attacker inside the container can do.',
    'The **Pod Security Standards** bundle these into levels — Privileged, Baseline, Restricted — and **Pod Security Admission** applies a level to a namespace by label. `enforce` rejects non-compliant Pods at creation. It does not touch running Pods, and it does not reject Deployments: their ReplicaSets simply fail to create replacements, which you discover at the next restart.',
    'So preview: `kubectl label --dry-run=server --overwrite ns freight pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted` lists the Pods that would violate and the checks they fail. That list is your to-do list for the manifest.',
    'Add the securityContext and roll out. Expect one surprise: with a read-only root filesystem the process can no longer write its cache, and exits with "read-only file system". Give it an `emptyDir` mounted at `/tmp` — writable paths become explicit. (An image that runs as root would fail earlier, with `CreateContainerConfigError`: runAsNonRoot verifies, it does not convert.) When the hardened Pods are Ready, set the label for real.',
  ],
  '5.6': [
    'Reviews catch mistakes sometimes; **admission control** catches them every time. After a request is authenticated and authorized, the API server runs it through admission plugins that may reject it before anything is stored. Quotas and Pod Security are built-in examples. A **ValidatingAdmissionPolicy** lets you add your own rule without running a webhook.',
    'Read the draft: `matchConstraints` says which resources and operations it sees; each `validations` entry is a **CEL** expression that must evaluate to true, with a `message` for humans. `object` is the incoming resource — `object.spec.template.spec.containers.all(c, !c.image.endsWith(\':latest\'))`. The policy does nothing until a **ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding** activates it, scopes it with a `namespaceSelector`, and chooses `validationActions`.',
    'Start in `Warn`. Apply the file and create a Deployment the rule dislikes: it is admitted, and kubectl prints a `Warning:`. That is how you discover what a rule would break before it breaks it.',
    'Then add the missing rule — `object.spec.replicas <= 10` — switch the binding to `Deny`, and test both directions: a bad Deployment and a wild `kubectl scale` are refused with your message; a normal change still goes through; and the billing namespace, which the selector does not match, is untouched. Signature verification needs more than CEL — that is where Kyverno or the Sigstore policy-controller come in — but the enforcement point is this same step.',
  ],
  '5.7': [
    'A capstone with four locks on one door, and the API server will tell you about them one at a time. Apply the manifest as it is and read `kubectl describe sts ledger -n vault`: zero Pods, and a `FailedCreate` event quoting the first refusal. Fix that, look again, read the next. This loop — apply, describe, fix — is the method.',
    'Identity first: the Pods ask for ServiceAccount `ledger`, which does not exist. Create it with a Role limited to ConfigMaps in vault and a RoleBinding. Then admission: the namespace enforces Restricted, so the container needs the full securityContext. Those are template changes; a StatefulSet accepts them.',
    'Storage is different. Each member needs its own disk, which means `volumeClaimTemplates` — a field that cannot be changed on an existing StatefulSet. Delete the StatefulSet (it has no data yet) and apply the corrected manifest. Finally the registry: create a `docker-registry` Secret with the pull credentials and attach it to the ServiceAccount\'s `imagePullSecrets`; a Pod already stuck in `ImagePullBackOff` picks it up on its next retry, or sooner if you delete it.',
    'Apply the image policy, wait for 3/3 — members start in order, each after the previous is Ready — and run the acceptance test: delete `ledger-0`, then read its logs. Same name, same claim, entries recovered. Durable, least-privileged, hardened, and only from the trusted path.',
  ],
  '6.1': [
    'The scheduler places each new Pod in two steps: **filter** out the nodes that cannot run it, then **score** the rest. Everything you write under `nodeSelector`, `affinity`, `tolerations` and `topologySpreadConstraints` feeds one of those steps. When no node survives the filter, the Pod stays `Pending` and its events list every node with the reason — `kubectl describe pod` is the whole diagnosis.',
    'The optimizer needs 6 CPUs; the general workers have 4. The batch node has room but carries a **taint**, `workload=batch:NoSchedule`, which repels every Pod that does not declare a matching **toleration**. A toleration is permission, not direction: add it and the Pod *may* land there. The ticket says *only* there, so also select the pool with `nodeSelector: {node.orbital.local/pool: batch}`. Dedicated nodes always need both halves.',
    'The frontend has the opposite problem: a leftover `nodeSelector` pins all replicas to one machine. Remove it and state the real intent with a **topology spread constraint**: `maxSkew: 1` over `topology.kubernetes.io/zone`, `whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule`. One catch — during the rollout the old replicas still match the selector and count; `matchLabelKeys: [pod-template-hash]` restricts the count to the new revision.',
    'Check the result with `kubectl get pods -n freight -o wide`: one frontend per dock. Then watch what that buys you when a dock goes dark — and notice what a *hard* constraint does to the replacement Pod.',
  ],
  '6.2': [
    'Traffic is not constant, and a replica count chosen by hand is wrong most of the day. A **HorizontalPodAutoscaler** closes the loop: every 15 seconds it reads a metric, compares it with your target and rewrites `replicas` on the Deployment — `desired = ceil(current × metric ÷ target)`. Create one with `kubectl autoscale deployment frontend --min=2 --max=8 --cpu-percent=60 -n freight`.',
    'Then look at it: `kubectl get hpa -n freight` shows `cpu: <unknown>/60%`. An HPA that cannot read its metric does nothing — silently, until the day you need it. `kubectl describe hpa frontend -n freight` explains: *missing request for cpu*. Utilization is a percentage **of the request**, and this Deployment declares none.',
    'Fix the denominator: `kubectl set resources deployment frontend --requests=cpu=150m -n freight`. After the rollout the TARGETS column shows a real number, served by **metrics-server** — the same source as `kubectl top pods`. Pick the request from what a replica really uses at a comfortable load; the target percentage is the head-room you keep on top of it.',
    'When the campaign starts, watch `kubectl get hpa,pods -n freight`. Scale-up is fast, and it still takes a sync period plus Pod start-up — some requests are lost while capacity catches up. That gap is why you keep head-room and a sensible `minReplicas`. Scale-down waits five minutes on purpose.',
  ],
  '6.3': [
    'Nodes need maintenance: firmware, kernel, Kubernetes itself. `kubectl drain` empties a node by cordoning it and then asking the API server to **evict** each Pod. Eviction is a request, not a kill: the API server first checks every **PodDisruptionBudget** that selects the Pod, and refuses if the eviction would take the application below its budget.',
    'So give cargo-api a budget: `kubectl create pdb cargo-api --selector=app=cargo-api --min-available=2 -n freight`. `kubectl get pdb -n freight` shows ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS: with three healthy replicas, one may go. A drain now removes one replica, is refused the next, and retries every five seconds until the replacement is Ready on another node. Two engineers draining at once can no longer take everything down.',
    'Start the drain: `kubectl drain orbital-worker-b --ignore-daemonsets`. If it keeps printing the same eviction error, read that list again. A budget of `minAvailable: 1` on a workload with one replica allows zero disruptions, always. The drain is not broken; it is obeying an impossible promise. Stop it with Ctrl+C.',
    'The honest fix depends on the workload: run a second replica, or admit that this one tolerates a short restart — `maxUnavailable: 1`. Change the budget, drain again, and uncordon the node when infra hands it back. A budget protects only against *voluntary* disruption; a node that dies asks nobody.',
  ],
  '6.4': [
    'A shared cluster needs fences. A **ResourceQuota** caps what one namespace may claim in total — CPU and memory requests and limits, object counts. It is enforced at admission: a Pod that would exceed the quota is not created at all. That is why billing sees no Pending Pods. The Deployment asked, the ReplicaSet tried, the API server said no.',
    'The refusal is recorded where the attempt was made: `kubectl describe rs -n billing` (or `kubectl get events -n billing`) shows `FailedCreate … exceeded quota`. `kubectl describe quota -n billing` shows Used against Hard. The invoicer requests a full CPU per replica and uses a fraction of it: right-size the requests in the manifest and all four replicas fit.',
    'report-worker fails differently: `must specify limits.cpu…`. Once a quota tracks a resource, every container has to declare it, and this manifest declares nothing. You could edit it — and the next manifest, and the one after. Better: a **LimitRange** that gives the namespace defaults. `defaultRequest` and `default` are written into each container at admission, before the quota is checked.',
    'One operational detail: the ReplicaSet controller retries a refused create with exponential back-off, up to about sixteen minutes, and nothing tells it that a LimitRange appeared. `kubectl rollout restart deployment/report-worker -n billing` makes it try now. Finish with `kubectl describe ns billing`: quota and limits, side by side.',
  ],
  '6.5': [
    'Kubernetes ships three minor releases a year and supports each for roughly fourteen months, so upgrading is routine work, not a project. The order is fixed: control plane first, then nodes, one minor version at a time. In between, the cluster runs with **version skew** — kubelets may lag the API server by up to three minors. `kubectl get nodes` shows where you are.',
    'What breaks in upgrades is rarely the cluster; it is the manifests. Beta API versions are removed on a published schedule. Objects already stored are converted by the server, but a YAML file in a repository still says `batch/v1beta1`, and `kubectl apply` answers `no matches for kind "CronJob" in version "batch/v1beta1"`. `kubectl api-resources` lists what the server serves today.',
    'For these three files the stable versions accept the same fields: change `apiVersion` to `batch/v1`, `policy/v1` and `autoscaling/v2`, and apply. That is not always so — check the deprecation guide for renamed fields before every upgrade.',
    'Now the node. With the frontend\'s PodDisruptionBudget in place, `kubectl drain orbital-worker-a --ignore-daemonsets` moves the workloads off politely. The automation upgrades the kubelet; when `kubectl get nodes` shows the new version, `kubectl uncordon` puts the node back in service. Repeat per node — which is exactly what a managed node-pool upgrade does for you.',
  ],
  '6.6': [
    'Peak day combines the chapter: demand you cannot predict, and maintenance you cannot postpone. The tools are the ones you have used separately — an autoscaler, a disruption budget, a drain. The skill is running them together without letting one undo the other.',
    'Start with the signal. CPU follows load loosely; requests per second *is* the load. In `manifests/hpa.yaml` replace the `Resource` metric with a `Pods` metric: `metric.name: http_requests_per_second`, `target.type: AverageValue`, `averageValue: "8"`. The HPA now holds an average of eight requests per second per replica. After applying, `kubectl get hpa -n freight` should show a value such as `6/8`; `<unknown>` would mean the adapter does not serve that metric name.',
    'Next the budget: `kubectl create pdb frontend --selector=app=frontend --max-unavailable=1 -n freight`. `maxUnavailable` is the better form for an autoscaled workload — it stays correct at three replicas and at ten, where a fixed `minAvailable` would not.',
    'When storage asks for orbital-worker-c, drain it: `kubectl drain orbital-worker-c --ignore-daemonsets`. Read the output as it runs: one eviction, a refusal, a retry once the replacement is Ready. Slower than deleting Pods, and that is the point — at peak you cannot spare two replicas at once. Leave the node cordoned and confirm with `kubectl get pods -n freight -o wide` that every replica is Ready elsewhere.',
  ],
  '7.1': [
    'Two environments, two folders of nearly identical YAML, and sooner or later a copy goes to the wrong place. **Kustomize** — built into kubectl — removes the copies. A **base** holds the manifests once. Each environment gets an **overlay**: a `kustomization.yaml` that points at the base and lists only the differences. The result is plain YAML; there is no template language to learn.',
    'Read the two files that exist: `cat deploy/base/kustomization.yaml deploy/overlays/staging/kustomization.yaml`. Then render without applying — `kubectl kustomize deploy/overlays/staging` — and compare the output with the base files: a namespace was set, a label added, and the ConfigMap got a hash in its name, with the Deployment\'s reference rewritten to match. `kubectl apply -k` does the same rendering and applies it.',
    'The prod overlay needs five statements: `namespace`, `resources: [../../base]`, `labels`, `images` with `newTag: 1.6.0`, and `replicas`. For the log level, add a `configMapGenerator` entry with the same name as the base\'s and `behavior: merge` — an overlay may adjust a generated ConfigMap, never redefine it silently. Render first, apply second.',
    'The hash is the clever part. When support asks for `debug`, change one literal and apply: the ConfigMap gets a new name, so the Pod template changes, so the Deployment rolls. Configuration changes become rollouts — visible, gradual, and undoable — instead of edits that running Pods never notice.',
  ],
  '7.2': [
    '**Helm** is the package manager of Kubernetes. A **chart** bundles templates and default values; installing it with your values creates a **release**, and Helm remembers every revision of that release inside the cluster. Most third-party software reaches a cluster this way, so you need to be fluent in five verbs: template, install, upgrade, history, rollback.',
    'Look before you install: `helm template notifier charts/notifier -f charts/values-prod.yaml` prints the manifests Helm would apply. The `{{ .Values.replicaCount }}` placeholders in `charts/notifier/templates/` are filled from `values.yaml`, overridden by your `-f` file, overridden again by `--set`. Then `helm install notifier charts/notifier -n freight -f charts/values-prod.yaml` and `helm list -n freight`.',
    'Upgrades are where Helm earns its keep, and where it surprises people twice. First: `helm upgrade` reports `deployed` the moment the API server accepts the manifests. It did not look at your Pods — `kubectl get pods` does. When they are stuck, `helm history notifier -n freight` shows the revisions and `helm rollback notifier 1 -n freight` re-applies a good one as a new revision.',
    'Second: values are not sticky. Every upgrade starts from the chart defaults plus what you pass *this time*. Omit `-f charts/values-prod.yaml` and production quietly returns to one replica. Pass the same value files on every upgrade (CI does this for you), and check with `helm get values notifier -n freight`.',
  ],
  '7.3': [
    'So far you changed the cluster by talking to it. **GitOps** inverts that: the desired state lives in a Git repository, and a controller inside the cluster — here **Argo CD** — continuously makes the cluster match. An Argo CD **Application** connects a repository path to a destination namespace. `kubectl get applications -n argocd` shows its two verdicts: *sync* (does live equal Git?) and *health* (do the workloads work?).',
    'This Application has `selfHeal` enabled, which explains yesterday\'s mystery. Try `kubectl scale deployment frontend --replicas=4 -n freight` and watch: within seconds Argo CD notices the drift and restores what Git says. The cluster did not ignore the command; it was corrected. In a GitOps setup kubectl is for looking, Git is for changing.',
    'So change Git. Edit `deploy/apps/frontend/deployment.yaml`, then in `~/deploy`: `git status`, `git add -A`, `git commit -m "…"`, `git push`. Argo CD polls the repository (every three minutes by default, twenty seconds here); `kubectl annotate application frontend -n argocd argocd.argoproj.io/refresh=normal` asks for a refresh now. The Application goes `Progressing`, then `Healthy`.',
    'The same path handles accidents. When a bad commit lands, the Application stays `Synced` — the cluster does match Git — but health drops. Do not fix the cluster. `git pull`, `git log --oneline` to find the commit, `git revert HEAD`, `git push`. The rollback is itself a reviewed, recorded change.',
  ],
  '7.4': [
    'Kubernetes knows Deployments and Services because their types are registered in the API. A **CustomResourceDefinition** registers a new type of your own — here `RouteCache` in group `orbital.local`. After `kubectl apply -f operator/crd.yaml` the API server stores, validates and serves RouteCaches like anything else: `kubectl api-resources --api-group=orbital.local`, `kubectl get routecaches`.',
    'Validation comes from the CRD\'s OpenAPI schema. Apply the freight team\'s manifest and the API server refuses it with the field and the allowed values — the same quality of error you get for a built-in kind. Fix the file and apply again. Now the object exists… and nothing happens, because a custom resource is only a record. Something has to act on it.',
    'That something is the **operator**: a controller, running as an ordinary Deployment, that watches RouteCaches and creates what they describe. Apply `operator/operator.yaml`. When a custom resource stays without status, read the controller: `kubectl logs deploy/route-operator -n platform`. This one is `forbidden` to list its own resource — operators are API clients and need RBAC like any other.',
    'Grant exactly what the ticket lists — `kubectl create clusterrole … --resource=routecaches.orbital.local,deployments.apps` and a ClusterRoleBinding to the operator\'s ServiceAccount. Seconds later the RouteCache reports `Ready`. From then on you manage the cache through the RouteCache: change `spec.replicas` there, and let the operator own the Deployment.',
  ],
  '7.5': [
    'This capstone hands production to the pipeline. The pieces are familiar: a Kustomize base and prod overlay in `~/deploy`, Argo CD in the cluster, and an Application manifest that connects them. Start there: `kubectl apply -f argocd/application.yaml`, then `kubectl get applications -n argocd`.',
    'If sync status is `Unknown`, Argo CD could not even compute the desired state. `kubectl describe application freight-prod -n argocd` shows a `ComparisonError` condition with the reason. Compare the `path` in the manifest with what exists in the repository (`ls overlays`), fix, re-apply. The Application adopts the running frontend without restarting it — the manifests already matched.',
    'Releasing is now a commit: set `newTag: 1.6.0` in `overlays/prod/kustomization.yaml`; retire the banner by removing its file and its line in `base/kustomization.yaml`. Render locally with `kubectl kustomize overlays/prod` before you commit — a broken kustomization in Git stops every later sync. Then `git add -A`, `git commit`, `git push`.',
    'Finally, the three switches. `automated: {}` only applies new commits. Objects removed from Git stay in the cluster until `prune: true`; manual changes stay until `selfHeal: true`. Set both on the Application. When someone deletes the prod Service by hand, that setting is the difference between an incident and a few seconds nobody notices.',
  ],
  '8.1': [
    'To Kubernetes a GPU is an **extended resource**: a countable thing a node advertises, here `nvidia.com/gpu`. A device plugin — installed by the NVIDIA GPU Operator together with drivers — reports how many the node has; `kubectl describe node orbital-gpu-1` lists them under Capacity and Allocatable. Pods request them under `resources.limits`, in whole numbers, and a GPU belongs to one container at a time.',
    'Start as always with the Pending Pod: `kubectl describe pod -n ml -l job-name=eta-train`. The scheduler\'s message has one clause per group of nodes. Three workers have no GPUs at all; the GPU node has an *untolerated taint*. Accelerator nodes are tainted so that ordinary Pods do not fill them — a GPU workload must carry the matching toleration.',
    'Add the toleration to `ml/train-job.yaml` and apply: the API server refuses, because a Job\'s Pod template is immutable. That is by design — a Job is a record of one run. Delete the Job and create it again. The Pod is still Pending, with a new reason: `Insufficient nvidia.com/gpu`.',
    'Now it is a capacity question. `kubectl describe node orbital-gpu-1` shows, under Allocated resources, two of two GPUs requested, and which Pods hold them. An idle notebook keeps its GPU exactly like a busy one; nothing reclaims it. Free one as agreed — `kubectl scale deployment notebook --replicas=1 -n ml` — and the Job is scheduled within a second. Confirm with `kubectl logs job/eta-train -n ml`.',
  ],
  '8.2': [
    'Most organizations give each team a namespace on a shared cluster rather than a cluster each. A namespace alone isolates nothing but names. A **tenant** is a namespace with four fences, and this ticket builds each one: who may act (RBAC), how much they may use (ResourceQuota and LimitRange), who may connect (NetworkPolicy), and what their Pods may do (Pod Security).',
    'Create the namespace and label it: `team=drones` for selectors, `pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=baseline` for Pod Security. Add the quota (`kubectl create quota … --hard=requests.cpu=2,…`) and a LimitRange with `defaultRequest` and `default` — without it, the first manifest that lacks resource settings is refused by the quota. `kubectl describe ns drones` shows both.',
    'Access: do not write a new Role. The built-in ClusterRole `edit` already describes a developer; a **RoleBinding** in the namespace grants it there and nowhere else: `kubectl create rolebinding drones-devs-edit --clusterrole=edit --group=drones-devs -n drones`. Test as a member with impersonation: `kubectl auth can-i create deployments -n drones --as=jo --as-group=drones-devs` — and the same in `freight`.',
    'Isolation needs two NetworkPolicies: one selecting every Pod with no ingress rules (default deny), one allowing ingress from `podSelector: {}` — which, inside a policy, means "any Pod in this namespace". Finally deploy the team\'s sample app as jo. If it runs, is reachable from its own namespace and not from freight, the tenant is ready — and the whole bundle belongs in Git.',
  ],
  '8.3': [
    'Every safeguard in this course rests on a claim: replicas survive a Pod loss, spreading survives a node loss. **Chaos engineering** tests such claims on purpose. The method is an experiment: name the steady state (95% of requests succeed), state a hypothesis, inject one failure with a small blast radius, watch, and fix what you learn. Read the plan first: `cat gameday/plan.md`.',
    'The tool here is **Chaos Mesh**, an operator like the one in Chapter 7. Each failure type is a custom resource; `gameday/pod-kill.yaml` is a `PodChaos` with `action: pod-kill`, `mode: one` and a selector for cargo-api Pods in freight. Applying it *is* running the experiment. `kubectl describe podchaos kill-one-cargo-api -n freight` shows which Pod was chosen.',
    'Run it against the system as it stands, and watch the traffic. The hypothesis fails: cargo-api has one replica, and for as long as its replacement takes to start, every request fails. That is the finding. The fix is the chapter-6 toolkit: three replicas, a topology spread constraint over `kubernetes.io/hostname`, and a PodDisruptionBudget for maintenance.',
    'Then repeat the experiment — `pod-kill` is one-shot, so delete the PodChaos and apply it again — and this time customers notice nothing. The node loss that follows tests the spread: with one replica per worker, any single machine costs a third of capacity and no availability. A game day ends with a written result for each hypothesis; the failed ones are the valuable ones.',
  ],
};
